3.2. Spatially discrete representation of the added mass operator
In order to work out the instability of the staggered coupled system the spatially discretised representations of the field Eqs. (1)–(3) are employed. Further some simplifications are made to ease the analysis.
• The discretisation of fluid and structure is conforming along the interface C. • Both field equations can be regarded as linear. • The influence of mesh motion and thus the temporal change of all significant matrices is negligible. • The structural density qS remains constant. • No external forces except the traction along C are considered. • No physical structural damping is assumed.
The first assumption simply eases the presentation while the other assumptions are reasonable as the considered instability typically occurs at very early time steps, i.e. when no significant nonlinearity has been built up. The assumption of linearity is required for an analysis of the maximal eigenvalue of an amplification operator at which we aim. Nevertheless the insight obtained also transfers to the nonlinear problem which is linearised in every time step. To further simplify the analysis the fluid stiffness is omitted in (21) at this point. This is reasonable as the instabilities we consider increase with decreasing time step. After time discretisation the discrete equations will be dominated by the mass and pressure terms when very small time steps are employed. Thus, this simplification allows to clearly highlight the reason of the instability. The simplified fluid system of equations is now split up into degrees of freedom belonging to the interior of the fluid domain and others at the interface. The assumption that the coefficient matrices do not change in time allows to use the ALE time derivative of the divergence Eq. (3)
