and will thus definitely become unstable for every density ratio if the simulation time is long enough. While ‘long enough’ is usually reached rather soon. Among the schemes considered here the trapezoidal rule (and more generally the implicit one-step-h method for all h < 1) yield such necessarily unstable schemes. But also the use of the correct second order boundary condition along the interface C (18) results in a scheme with fully recursive characteristics which is thus unstable after a small number of steps. In particular we observe that the maximal eigenvalue maxi li is larger than one while the instability constant C (or also C/n) turns out smaller than one for most schemes of practical interest. Thus stable staggered simulations of problems with comparable mass densities are not possible. In particular blood flow-artery interaction or membranewater interaction immediately yield unstable staggered schemes. Problems with very low fluid density compared to the structure however can be conditionally stable. Thus the instability of sequentially staggered schemes is typically not observed when aerodynamics are considered. A derivation of a discrete representation of the added mass operator reveals that while in an unstabilised case this operator is purely geometrical in the stabilised case it depends upon the fluid density and more importantly also on the time step size. Numerical investigations confirm that the maximal eigenvalue of the added mass operator of a discretisation with stabilised fluid elements increases at deceasing time step thus explaining the particularly destabilising effect observed in the context of stabilised incompressible flow and very small time steps. For schemes with stationary characteristics stability at small time steps might be restored by ignoring the stabilisation matrix GT
M
which decreases the maximal eigenvalue of the added mass operator and introduces a consistency error. Nevertheless most practical schemes exhibit instationary characteristics and thus get unstable irrespective of the eigenvalues of MA after a limited number of steps. In the context of stabilised fluid formulations and to a lower extent also for inf–sup stable elements due to the influence of the stiffnesses the artificial added mass effect imposes a lower bound on the time step for schemes which allow for conditionally stable computations. An upper bound on the time step results from the CFL condition introduced by the explicit character of the sequential cou
pling. Thus for practical applications the range of allowed time step sizes might be rather small or even empty due to an overlap of both restrictions. There is further a limit density ratio for every scheme irrespective of the actual representation of the added mass operator which provides an ultimate instability condition. Thus no sequentially staggered scheme can be constructed which would be unconditionally stable with respect to the density ratio. The actual parameters at which the instability will occur are hard to predict precisely especially when stabilised fluid elements are employed and significant nonlinearities have to be considered. However the relationship between different time discretisation schemes with respect to their influence on the instability provides some information which applies also to real FSI calculations. Numerical investigations further indicate that for FSI calculations coupling slender structures with incompressible flow the density ratio at which stable simulations can be performed is very restrictive. Thus the conclusion drawn by Mok [1–3] is justified; iteratively staggered schemes are to be preferred within most such FSI calculations.
